  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nA daily chance of showers and thunderstorms is in the forecast \ntoday and Friday. With this, a few strong to severe thunderstorms \nare possible during the afternoon and evening both days, generally\neast of Billings.\n\nToday, there is a Marginal Risk (1/5) of severe thunderstorms \nover much of eastern Montana and north-central/eastern Wyoming \nwith a Slight Risk (2/5) near and east of the Montana and Wyoming \nstate line. The Slight Risk (2/5) area covers thunderstorms that \nare expected to initiate off a convergence zone near the Montana \nand Wyoming state line during the afternoon. Should these \nthunderstorms develop over far eastern Montana, the initial threat\nis large hail as thunderstorms quickly strengthen and move east \ninto North Dakota and South Dakota. Localized strong winds and \nheavy rain cannot be ruled out as well. The Marginal Risk (1/5) \narea covers an additional round of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms that is expected to develop over the Bighorn Basin \nand lift north and east over the mountains and plains of northern \nWyoming and eastern Montana during the late afternoon and evening.\nWith decent instability (CAPE values around 500 to 1000 J/kg), \nany thunderstorms that develop with this round should strengthen \nover the plains, but limited shear will inhibit organization and \nupscale growth. Therefore, the main threat is strong wind gusts, \nthough isolated hail and heavy rain remain possible. Outside of \nthis activity, there is a low chance of morning showers over far \nsouth- eastern Montana this morning as a low level jet develops. \n\nFriday, isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms are \nforecast across the area during the afternoon and evening, with a \nlow chance of morning showers over far south-eastern Montana once \nagain. With this, the risk of severe weather is greatest over \neastern Montana, east of Billings, where there is a Marginal Risk \n(1/5) for severe thunderstorms. With similar severe parameters to \nThursday, instability will favor a few strong thunderstorms but \nlimited shear should inhibit organization and upscale growth. With\nthat said, strong winds, hail, a brief heavy rain are the main \nthreats, slightly favoring strong winds. \n\nMostly dry and hotter conditions return this weekend as low \namplitude ridging builds into the area. Look for high temperatures\nto return to the upper 80s to lower 90s during this time. \n\nEarly next week will see hot temperatures continue, though a \ndaily low chance of showers and potentially a few thunderstorms \nreturns. Depending on how warm temperatures get, temperatures \naloft may inhibit this potential during this time, but there does \nlook to be enough atmospheric moisture around to support the \ndaily low chance of precipitation. On that note, the chance of \nsevere thunderstorms is low as well. LA",
